{
  // Markdown validation rules — deliberately PER PROJECT, not in VS Code's user settings, so
  // corporate repos never inherit them. The global user-settings block owns the preview
  // cosmetics (stylesheet, font, line height, wrap); the rules that produce warnings live here.
  //
  // These settings are an ADVISORY SUBSET of the push gate, never a second opinion. The gate is
  // `lychee --include-fragments .` plus the case-exactness guard (wonder-queen D-025); it is a
  // git hook, so it has always been editor-agnostic and VS Code has never been in that path.
  //
  // referenceLinks is OFF so the editor matches the gate. lychee parses real CommonMark, where
  // an undefined shortcut reference is a text node rather than a link — so it has no such rule
  // and structurally cannot have one. This is alignment, not a workaround. The rule it replaced
  // produced 301 of 326 fleet warnings with zero true positives, all ordinary bracketed prose
  // ([CRITICAL], [bot], [--base <branch>]), and buried real findings beneath them.
  "markdown.validate.referenceLinks.enabled": "ignore",

  // These two stay on: they are the editor-side echo of what the gate enforces, and between them
  // they found all 16 real defects in the fleet — links left behind by a file move, a doubled
  // path prefix, and a fragment pointing at a renumbered heading. All 16 are fixed.
  "markdown.validate.fileLinks.enabled": "warning",
  "markdown.validate.fragmentLinks.enabled": "warning"
}
